[start related work 1]
[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Chaput et al., 2016-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/fe7697844facb2a5106131b4d876513a2d8afba3)
- venue: Journal of Cheminformatics
- citation count: 111
[method]
The paper used the DUD-E benchmark to evaluate structure-based virtual screening. It prepared 102 protein targets with 4,000 active compounds and 40,000 decoys, corresponding to 400 decoys per target, defined binding sites from crystal structures, prepared the protein and ligand inputs, and docked each target with GOLD, Glide, Surflex, and FlexX. Performance was assessed with docking enrichment, net balance, and BEDROC scores, and the results were correlated with protein target characteristics, target families, binding-site properties, protein structure quality, small-molecule descriptors, and 2D similarity between actives and the crystal ligand to identify chemical library bias. This is related to the proposed BRAF LBVS plan because the plan uses DUD-E decoys as inactives or spike-ins and aims to build a high-quality benchmark; the paper shows how decoy selection, active-decoy similarity, and library bias can affect virtual-screening performance.
[results]
GOLD gave the best overall docking performance, followed by Glide, Surflex, and FlexX. The reported overall BEDROC and 1% enrichment values were approximately 0.62 and 3.0 for GOLD, with lower values for Glide, Surflex, and FlexX, approximately 0.58 and 2.6, 0.55 and 2.4, and 0.52 and 2.2, respectively. Performance was strongly target-dependent: targets with chemical library bias and actives similar to the crystal ligand produced higher enrichment, while removing such biases reduced the apparent differences among programs and left only modest enrichment. The quantitative benchmark scale was 102 targets, 4,000 actives, and 40,000 decoys, with target-specific enrichment and BEDROC values rather than a single global score.
[context]
Key experimental conditions were 102 targets, 4,000 actives, 40,000 decoys, 400 decoys per target, four docking programs, target-specific binding sites derived from crystal structures, and evaluation by enrichment and BEDROC rather than classification accuracy. The analysis included protein families, binding-site properties, protein structure quality, molecular descriptors, and 2D similarity to the crystal ligand. For the proposed BRAF dataset, this context is important because DUD-E decoys are not experimentally validated inactives; using them as inactives or spike-ins can introduce chemical library and similarity biases. The BRAF curation should therefore use explicit IC50-based actives, carefully selected true inactives, avoid inactives that resemble known BRAF actives or share library bias, and evaluate ranking performance with BEDROC or enrichment in addition to accuracy, precision, and recall.
