[method]
The paper used a controlled benchmarking protocol for positive-unlabeled learning on protein-protein interaction prediction. It constructed 27 Escherichia coli gene-expression datasets by treating known interacting protein pairs as positives, randomly moving a fraction of positives into an unlabeled set, and treating all other protein pairs as unlabeled negatives. For each positive-to-total-positive ratio from 10% to 90%, it generated three random datasets with 250 total positives and 1,250 negatives, giving a fixed 5:1 negative-to-positive ratio. It then evaluated eight PU learning algorithms, including SVM only, Carter, PSoL original and modified, Bagging SVM, AGPS, PosOnly, Roc-SVM, and S-EM, using 10-fold cross-validation where applicable, LIBSVM C-SVC with an RBF kernel, and precision, recall, F-measure, and Matthews correlation coefficient. This protocol is related to the proposed BRAF virtual-screening method because both use multiple randomly generated, ratio-controlled training partitions and repeated evaluation to reduce training bias and make data-composition effects comparable; the key difference is that the paper’s unlabeled set contains hidden positives, while the proposed plan uses curated inactives and a separate hold-out active set.
[results]
The provided excerpt does not include the numeric values from the performance tables, so quantitative algorithm rankings cannot be reported. The reported outcomes are qualitative: SVM only generally performed worst on F-measure because it labeled almost all unlabeled examples negative, producing high precision but low recall; Carter had good recall but very low precision because it labeled most unlabeled examples positive; original PSoL had the second-lowest F-measure, also labeling most unlabeled examples negative; modified PSoL improved recall and F-measure by increasing correctly classified positives and decreasing incorrectly labeled negatives. Wilcoxon signed-rank tests for F-measure and Matthews correlation coefficient were performed and p-values were placed in tables, but the p-values are not shown in the provided text.
[context]
The data consisted of 445 microarray expression profiles for 4,345 Escherichia coli genes; each protein-pair feature vector had length 890. Known interactions were taken from the IntAct database. The evaluation used three random datasets per ratio, averaged results, and used 10-fold cross-validation for SVM only and Roc-SVM. Hyperparameters included Bagging SVM T=20 and K=|P|; PSoL original per-iteration extraction limit T1=|P|*3 and modified total limit T2=|P|*4; LIBSVM C-SVC RBF kernel with c=1.0/128 and C=1000 for two-class SVMs; AGPS one-class SVM with RBF kernel and default parameters; S-EM run with LPU software using spy and EM options. No hardware or runtime specifications are given. The protocol is relevant to the proposed data-centric plan’s emphasis on reproducible, bias-controlled dataset construction, but it is not directly transferable because the paper evaluates PU learning with unlabeled positives rather than fully labeled actives and inactives for ligand-based virtual screening.
