[method]
The paper's related method is the construction and bias analysis of the DUD-E docking benchmark. It prepared 102 protein targets from crystal structures, defined binding sites, collected validated actives, and selected drug-like decoys as inactives. The compounds were docked with GOLD, Glide, Surflex, and FlexX, and performance was evaluated with docking enrichment, net balance, and BEDROC. The authors then stratified results by target family, binding-site properties, ligand properties, and similarity to the crystal-structure ligand, and performed a bias-removal analysis to determine what program differences remained after accounting for target, ligand, and chemical-library biases. This is related to the proposed BRAF training/hold-out design because both involve curated actives/inactives and explicit control of dataset biases, but the paper does not create ML training sets, hold-out actives, or balanced non-overlapping inactives for supervised learning.
[results]
DUD-E contains 102 targets and 415,951 compounds, comprising 102,494 actives and 313,457 decoys. Docking performance was strongly target- and ligand-dependent, and the four programs did not show uniform dominance across all targets. The bias-removal analysis showed that chemical-library biases were necessary but not sufficient for good performance of Gold, Surflex, and FlexX, and that after removing target, ligand, and library biases the programs' performance differences narrowed, indicating that much of the apparent performance variation was driven by dataset composition rather than intrinsic docking ability. Statistical significance values and exact per-program BEDROC/enrichment values are not specified in the summarized method.
[context]
The benchmark used crystal-structure-defined binding sites, drug-like decoys selected to reduce similarity to actives, and four docking programs (GOLD, Glide, Surflex, FlexX). Evaluation used docking enrichment, net balance, and BEDROC scores across 102 targets. Computational resources and software versions are not specified in the summarized method. The analysis was a docking benchmark, not a supervised ML training/hold-out experiment; therefore it does not directly validate the 3,600/500 BRAF active split or the five balanced training datasets, but it provides a precedent for curating actives/inactives and removing dataset biases before performance evaluation.
