[method]
The paper evaluated a ligand-only virtual screening baseline on the DUD-E benchmark. For each small molecule, a 4096-bit Extended Connectivity Fingerprint was computed, and target or protein-pocket information was deliberately ignored. A logistic regression classifier was then trained to distinguish active compounds from decoys. The benchmark was partitioned at the target level, with 72 of 102 targets used for training and 30 held-out targets used for testing, corresponding to a 70% training and 30% testing split. This method is related to the proposed BRAF ligand-based virtual screening plan because it uses the same general task of classifying actives versus inactives, employs a fingerprint type included in the proposed representation set, uses a conventional machine learning classifier, and directly examines whether apparent virtual screening performance may be driven by inactive-compound composition rather than target-specific learning.
[results]
The ECFP plus logistic regression model achieved a mean AUC of 0.904 on the 30 held-out DUD-E targets. This outperformed the other methods reported in the paper's DUD-E comparison: Smina achieved 0.700 mean AUC, AtomNet achieved 0.855 mean AUC, and DeepVS achieved 0.800 mean AUC. The paper did not report confidence intervals, p-values, or other statistical significance tests. The authors interpreted the high performance of a compound-only model as evidence that DUD-E decoys may be too similar across targets, allowing a classifier to separate actives from decoys without effectively learning target-specific binding relationships.
[context]
The DUD-E benchmark contained 102 targets, 22,886 active compounds, and more than 1 million decoys. The training and test sets contained different targets, but the active and decoy compounds were not balanced in the way proposed for the BRAF study. No protein structure, binding pocket, or target descriptor was used in this experiment. Hardware, software versions, and computational runtime were not reported. The result is relevant to the proposed research plan because it highlights a key data-composition risk: if inactives or decoys are highly similar or biased, even simple ligand-based models can appear strong. This supports the proposed method's emphasis on carefully curated BRAF inactives, balanced training sets, a fixed 500-compound active hold-out set, and five training datasets with non-overlapping inactive sets to reduce training bias and improve reproducibility.
