- in_text_citation: [(Cortes, and Vapnik, 1995-09)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/52b7bf3ba59b31f362aa07f957f1543a29a4279e)
- venue: Machine-mediated learning
- citation count: 48359
[method]
The paper implements support-vector network classifiers using optimal and soft-margin hyperplane construction. For binary classification, it solves a quadratic programming problem over Lagrange multipliers with constraints that enforce nonnegative multipliers and label balance, using a kernel matrix formed from dot products in a feature space. Kernels satisfying Mercer's condition are used, with polynomial kernels of specified degree as the main experimental feature-space transformations. The classifier is represented compactly by support vectors, and an incremental algorithm processes training data in portions, retaining support vectors and previously violated constraints to update the solution. For multi-class digit recognition, ten one-versus-all classifiers are constructed, one per digit, using the same kernel and preprocessing, and an unknown pattern is assigned to the class with the largest classifier output. The paper also constructed artificial two-class planar pattern sets and used quadratic kernels to visualize support vectors and errors. This method is related to the proposed BRAF ligand-based virtual screening plan because the proposed plan also includes support vector machines among five conventional machine-learning algorithms and systematically varies feature representations, here molecular fingerprints and paired fingerprints, whereas the paper systematically varies kernel-defined feature spaces and compares classifier performance against other learning machines. The relation is methodological rather than domain-specific, since the paper does not use molecular fingerprints, curated actives/inactives, or data-centric perturbations.
[results]
On the US Postal Service digit database, polynomial support-vector classifiers of degrees 1 through 7 were evaluated. The paper reports that test performance changed little as polynomial degree and feature-space dimensionality increased, indicating no obvious overfitting. Support-vector counts increased slowly with degree; the 7th-degree classifier had about 30% more support vectors than the 3rd-degree classifier and fewer than the linear classifier. The generalization bound held using the observed support-vector count, with an upper bound on single-classifier error probability not exceeding 3%, while actual single-classifier test error did not exceed 1.5%. For the linear classifier, the reported 200 support vectors included nonzero slack vectors, with an average of 34 training misclassifications per classifier; a 2nd-degree classifier reduced total training misclassifications to 4. Polynomial classifiers of degree 2 or higher outperformed the specialized LeNet1 network, which had 5.1% raw error. The paper's comparison table also lists raw errors of 2.5% for human performance, 17% for CART, 16% for C4.5, and 6.6% for the best two-layer neural network. On the NIST benchmark, a 4th-degree polynomial support-vector network with no preprocessing achieved 1.1% combined test error across ten classifiers, with average training error of 0.02%, about 12 misclassified training patterns per class, and 14 misclassified test patterns for classifier 1. Training time was reported to depend on support-vector count rather than polynomial degree and to be faster than the specialized LeNet1 network even in the worst case. For the planar examples, the reported solutions were optimal among second-degree polynomials, meaning no second-degree polynomial made fewer errors, and support patterns were a small fraction of training patterns; no numeric error rates are provided. No statistical significance tests are reported in the supplied text.
[context]
The experiments used two digit-recognition datasets: a US Postal Service set with 7,300 training and 2,000 test 16x16 pixel patterns, and a NIST mixture with 60,000 training and 10,000 test 28x28 pixel patterns, giving 784 input dimensions for NIST. US Postal Service experiments used centering, de-slanting, and Gaussian smoothing with standard deviation 0.75, and tested polynomial kernels of degrees 1 through 7. NIST experiments used only a 4th-degree polynomial kernel and no preprocessing because the benchmark was limited to two weeks. The training data were nonseparable, so soft-margin constraints and slack variables were used; support vectors could include misclassified or slack-carrying training vectors. The planar examples used two classes shown as black and white bullets, with support patterns marked by double circles and errors by crosses. The paper does not report hardware, software runtime, detailed hyperparameter search settings, or cross-validation; it uses fixed training and test splits. Unlike the proposed method, which plans 1,375 models across 55 molecular representations with 10-fold cross-validation and accuracy, precision, and recall, the paper evaluates a smaller set of kernel degrees and one-versus-all classifiers on image pixels, using raw error, support-vector counts, and generalization bounds as the main metrics.
