The paper trained a deep neural network for 12 Tox21 toxicity tasks using molecular representations including ECFP4 fingerprints, chemical descriptors, and toxicophore features. Inputs were preprocessed with standard deviation or tanh scaling, ECFP4 scaled by tanh or sqrt, sparse features thresholded, and feature subsets tested. The network used dropout, L2 weight decay, early stopping via cross-validation, and was trained with stochastic gradient descent in mini-batches of 512 on a single 12 GB GPU, with sparse input storage converted to dense on GPU. Evaluation used AUC on Tox21 leaderboard and private test sets, with cross-validation folds selected to match test label coverage and avoid structurally similar train/test leakage. It also compared single-task and multi-task networks. This relates to the proposed BRAF LBVS study because both evaluate how molecular representations and model architecture affect predictive performance in cheminformatics, but the paper uses a deep multi-task toxicity model and AUC rather than the proposed grid of conventional ML algorithms, fingerprint combinations, and accuracy/precision/recall for ligand-based virtual screening.
[results]
On the Tox21 leaderboard set, multi-task learning outperformed single-task learning for most tasks; mean AUCs improved from 0.8487 to 0.8409 for NR.AhR (not significant), 0.3755 to 0.3459 for NR.AR (not significant), 0.8799 to 0.9289 for NR.AR.LBD (p=0.011), 0.7523 to 0.7921 for NR.Aromatase (p=0.006), 0.6659 to 0.6949 for NR.ER (p=0.006), 0.6532 to 0.7272 for NR.ER.LBD (p=0.006), 0.6367 to 0.7102 for NR.PPAR.gamma (p=0.006), 0.7927 to 0.8017 for SR.ARE (p=0.148), 0.7972 to 0.7958 for SR.ATAD5 (p=0.338), 0.7354 to 0.8101 for SR.HSE (p=0.006), 0.8485 to 0.8489 for SR.MMP (p=0.265), and 0.6955 to 0.7487 for SR.p53 (p=0.006). In the final Tox21 challenge, the method achieved an average AUC of 0.846, NR panel average 0.826, and SR panel average 0.858, ranking first overall and in both panels, with task AUCs ranging from 0.793 to 0.941 and never placing lower than fifth. These results show that fingerprint- and descriptor-based molecular representations can support strong toxicity prediction, but they do not directly establish the relative performance of the proposed conventional ML algorithms or fingerprint combinations for BRAF ligand-based virtual screening.
[context]
The Tox21 dataset contained about 12,000 compounds across 12 binary toxicity tasks, split into 11,764 training compounds, 297 leaderboard compounds, and 643 private test compounds. Data cleaning removed redundant compounds and fragments, yielding 8,695 distinct compound fragments, and ChemAxon standardization was applied before feature calculation. The model handled up to 40,000 input features and large hidden layers, used sparse storage because dense input would require about 5 TB, and was trained on one GPU with 12 GB RAM. Hyperparameters were selected by cross-validation optimizing AUC per task, with folds constrained to compounds labeled on at least eight tasks and distributed by structural similarity clusters. The experimental setting is a multi-task toxicity benchmark with AUC evaluation, whereas the proposed plan uses a curated BRAF active/inactive dataset of about 4,100 actives and 24,000 inactives, balanced training sets, 10-fold cross-validation, and conventional ML metrics, so the paper provides related but not directly comparable evidence.
[end related work 3]

