[method]
The paper uses a deep neural network for Tox21 toxicity prediction and tests whether combining molecular feature types improves performance, which is related to the proposed comparison of standalone and merged molecular fingerprints and multi-view representation learning. Inputs included binary ECFP4 fingerprints, chemical descriptors, and toxicophore features. The authors tried feature subsets and combinations, including only binary ECFP4 fingerprints and combinations of chemical descriptors with toxicophore features. Preprocessing used standard deviation scaling or tanh for chemical descriptors, tanh or sqrt scaling for ECFP4, and thresholding to remove very sparse features. The network was regularized with dropout, small L2 weight decay, and early stopping selected by cross-validation. A multi-task network shared learning across the twelve Tox21 tasks, and single-task networks were trained as baselines. Evaluation used AUC on the Tox21 leaderboard set and final held-out test set. This is not a direct test of the proposed fingerprint list, but it addresses the same data-representation question by comparing single feature views with combined feature views and task-shared learning.
[results]
On the Tox21 leaderboard set, multi-task learning outperformed single-task learning in most tasks. Mean AUC over five random initializations improved significantly for NR.AR.LBD from 0.8799 to 0.9289 with p=0.011, NR.Aromatase from 0.7523 to 0.7921 with p=0.006, NR.ER from 0.6659 to 0.6949 with p=0.006, NR.ER.LBD from 0.6532 to 0.7272 with p=0.006, NR.PPAR.gamma from 0.6367 to 0.7102 with p=0.006, SR.HSE from 0.7354 to 0.8101 with p=0.006, and SR.p53 from 0.6955 to 0.7487 with p=0.006. Improvements were not statistically significant for NR.AhR, NR.AR, SR.ARE, SR.ATAD5, and SR.MMP, and both approaches failed on NR.AR because the leaderboard set contained only three positive examples. In the final Tox21 challenge, the method achieved the best average AUC of 0.846, the best Nuclear Receptor panel average of 0.826, and the best Stress Response panel average of 0.858, and it was reported as the overall winner.
[context]
The Tox21 Data Challenge dataset contained approximately 12,000 compounds across twelve tasks, split into 11,764 training compounds, 297 leaderboard compounds, and 643 private test compounds. After cleaning redundant carrier-containing records and fragment-like structures, the authors identified 8,695 distinct compound fragments and applied ChemAxon cleanup. Cross-validation used five folds, included only compounds labeled on at least eight tasks to approximate the final test label distribution, and distributed structurally similar compound clusters across folds to avoid overly easy splits. The network handled up to 40,000 input features and large hidden layers, was stored on a single GPU with 12 GB RAM, used mini-batches of 512, and converted sparse input batches to dense format on the GPU rather than using sparse matrix multiplication. Hyperparameters were optimized independently for each task using AUC as the criterion. Although the paper uses a deep network and ECFP4 rather than the proposed conventional ML algorithms and fingerprint set, it is relevant to the research plan because it demonstrates that molecular representation and combined feature views can materially change predictive performance.
