[method]
The paper used a deep neural network for Tox21 toxicity prediction, with up to 40,000 input features constructed from binary ECFP4 fingerprints, chemical descriptors, and toxicophore features. Input preprocessing included tanh or sqrt scaling for ECFP4 features, standard-deviation or tanh scaling for chemical descriptors, and thresholding to remove very sparse features. The network was regularized with dropout and L2 weight decay, and early stopping was selected through cross-validation. Implementation used sparse input storage, conversion to dense format on a single 12 GB GPU, and mini-batch stochastic gradient descent with batch size 512. Both single-task and multi-task networks were trained on the 12 Tox21 tasks, with hyperparameters tuned per task using 5-fold cross-validation. Cross-validation folds were restricted to compounds labeled on at least eight tasks and were balanced by distributing structurally similar compounds across folds. AUC was used as the optimization and evaluation metric. This method is relevant to the proposed research method because it is a published sophisticated deep-learning cheminformatics result that can serve as a prior model-centric benchmark when comparing data-centric conventional ML approaches, although it addresses toxicity prediction rather than BRAF ligand-based virtual screening.
[results]
On the Tox21 leaderboard set, multi-task learning outperformed single-task learning for most tasks, with results averaged over five random initializations and significance assessed by a two-sided Mann-Whitney U test. Notable improvements included NR.AR.LBD AUC 0.9289 versus 0.8799 with p=0.011, NR.Aromatase 0.7921 versus 0.7523 with p=0.006, NR.ER 0.6949 versus 0.6659 with p=0.006, NR.ER.LBD 0.7272 versus 0.6532 with p=0.006, NR.PPAR.gamma 0.7102 versus 0.6367 with p=0.006, SR.HSE 0.8101 versus 0.7354 with p=0.006, and SR.p53 0.7487 versus 0.6955 with p=0.006. Multi-task learning underperformed on NR.AR, with AUC 0.3459 versus 0.3755, a task with only three positive examples in the leaderboard set. On the final private test set of 643 compounds, the deep network achieved an average AUC of 0.846, an NR panel AUC of 0.826, and an SR panel AUC of 0.858. Individual task AUCs were AhR 0.928, AR 0.807, AR-LBD 0.850, ARE 0.840, Aromatase 0.834, ATAD5 0.793, ER 0.793, ER-LBD 0.814, HSE 0.858, MMP 0.941, p53 0.862, and PPAR.gamma 0.839. The approach was reported as the best average performer and winner of the NR, SR, and overall Tox21 challenge.
[context]
The Tox21 challenge data contained approximately 12,000 compounds across 12 tasks, split into 11,764 training compounds, 297 leaderboard compounds, and 643 private test compounds. After fragment cleaning and ChemAxon standardization, 8,695 distinct compound fragments were identified. The tasks comprised seven nuclear receptor and five stress response assays, with stress response tasks showing higher mutual correlation. The computational setting used a single GPU with 12 GB RAM, sparse storage because dense input would require about 5 TB, and mini-batch SGD with batch size 512. Cross-validation was designed to approximate the label density of the final test set and to reduce leakage from structurally similar compounds. For the proposed research plan, the key constraint is that these results come from a toxicity-prediction benchmark rather than a BRAF virtual-screening dataset, so they provide an external deep-learning performance reference rather than a direct same-target baseline.
