ng, although its assays were smaller and often unbalanced. Then [(Chávez-Hernández et al., 2023-06)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d9d24516c346d5287f4826245bd0c688f4533822) supports the curation workflow by recommending duplicate removal, SMILES canonicalization, salt and metal removal, optional PAINS/ADMET filtering, and balanced active/inactive sets, and by reporting that ChEMBL V.29 was approximately 71% active and 31% inactive, which motivates deliberate construction of a large inactive pool. The weakest but still relevant support is [(Chen et al., 2019-03)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/e66e075c1787824175966ce86488ca6c1e2cff2e), which found ligand-only CNN models equivalent to receptor-ligand models and evaluated ligand-fingerprint KNN baselines on PDBbind-derived data, supporting the feasibility of LBVS and KNN baselines, but it provides limited quantitative detail and is not BRAF-specific.",
